{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>טוב</b>, <b>בחיק</b>, <b>טוב</b> — three [instances of the word] joined together. [verses 32–33, 17:1]" "<b>טוב</b> — stands in place of two." "<b>וארך אפים</b> — He is better than a warrior, for he conquers his anger, but the warrior, even though he conquers others, cannot conquer his anger." "<b>מושל ברוחו</b> — by himself he is better than one who captures a city, who needs the help of others. And when the lot is cast בחיק, and from HaShem is its judgment, how then does it fall from HaShem upon one who can rule his spirit?"], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra indicates that the word טוב appears in three consecutive contexts: 16:32, 16:33 (where בחיק occurs), and 17:1 (which again opens with טוב). The bracketed reference appears to be a marginal notation listing these locations.", 1 "Meaning the word טוב at the head of v. 32 (טוב ארך אפים) encompasses two comparisons that follow: better than a warrior, and better than one who captures a city.", 3 "The phrase 'איך יפול כן מאת השם' is textually difficult; rendered hyper-literally. The argument links v. 33 (the lot cast בחיק) to v. 32, possibly suggesting a thematic connection between divine providence and self-mastery, but the syntax is compressed and possibly corrupt."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:23:20.570141922Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}